{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310630,"nonce":"kHiAtHTD10m6tJO40mmjpCeLInk=","title":"nue llama-chat load traps — import-compiled stdlib has fragile, order-dependent cross-module member resolution; partial fixes landed, root ...","message":"nue `llama-chat` load SIGILLs at `[load] reading GGUF` (2026-06-22). The trap is `GGUFReader`/`GGUFLoader` compiling to empty MIR → forward-ref trap. Root chain (all \"member of module B not resolvable while module A is import-compiled\"):\n\n- `GGUFReader` ← `StringTools.hex` ← `StringTools` fails on `'X'.code` (char-literal magic is NEVER folded → falls through to String field resolution → resolved only via `global_lookup` over String's globals at hir_to_mir.rs:26469).\n- `StringIterator`/`StringIteratorUnicode` ← `String.unsafeCodeAt` (real extern method).\n- `FPHelper` ← `Math.POSITIVE_INFINITY`; `haxe.io.Input` ← `FPHelper`; `haxe.Int64` fails on its own `high` abstract-property getter (copy() `make(high,low)`); `Int32` fails on `this = clamp(...)` (abstract reassigns this).\n\n**LANDED FIXES (committed):** multi-pass import retry + loud-fail (2c8f45bf); extern/@:native abstracts may omit underlying type → fixes Ptr/Usize/Ref/Box (851390cb); define `eval` so root stdlib `#if` picks native branches → fixes Math (`#if !eval` JS `Number[\"NaN\"]` bootstrap was compiling) + StringTools in isolation (b99567da, escape RAYZOR_NO_EVAL_DEFINE).\n\n**THE REMAINING ROOT IS ARCHITECTURAL + FRAGILE.** Each import module compiles in its OWN per-module builder; cross-module member access (`String.code`, `String.unsafeCodeAt`, `Math.POSITIVE_INFINITY`) resolves only if the dependency's members happen to be registered/visible at the exact pass the importer compiles. Whether that holds depends on **import-set composition + order**, so the bug is order-sensitive, not deterministic per-symbol:\n- `/tmp/imptest` (Main imports user `Helper` that uses StringTools), BARE: StringTools COMPILES.\n- same + a `rayzor.toml` with `class-paths=[\".\"]`: StringTools FAILS (the class-path pulls the user module into `load_imports_efficiently`, shifting order). **This is why nue (which has a rayzor.toml) fails but a bare file may not** — confirmed darmie's \"cross-module import resolution when using rayzor toml\" lead.\n- `/tmp/imptest3` (Main uses StringTools DIRECTLY, no user module), BARE: StringTools FAILS even without toml.\n\nFast repro (no GGUF): `/tmp/imptest` + a `rayzor.toml [build] class-paths=[\".\"]`, `rayzor run --no-cache Main.hx`. Diagnostics: RAYZOR_E0100_DEBUG=1 (shows `field='code' receiver=String kind=String` reaching the not-registered path in BOTH cases; the retry recovers it only when String's globals are registered first).\n\n**bc459055** (\"skip redundant compilation of extern-only stdlib\": Math/String/Array/Std/...) is a *partial* regression (skips member registration) but NOT the demonstrated failing path — there `String` is `loaded=false` → normal compile, not the skip. A metadata-only \"register members, discard MIR\" fix at the skip point was built + REVERTED (inert for the failing path; force-compiling extern-only files instead SIGSEGVs the runtime — placeholder bodies shadow real externs).\n\n**UPDATE 2026-06-22 (session cont.): char-magic `'X'.code` fold LANDED** (commit \"fix(tast): fold `'X'.code`...\") — `'X'.code` was NEVER folded (no compiler impl despite String.hx:70 doc); it fell through to String field resolution and only \"resolved\" via a fragile `g.name.ends_with(\".code\")` suffix match. Folded at ast_lowering.rs Field arm (~7779) by synthesizing `ExprKind::Int(c as i64)` + recursing. **This fixed StringTools → GGUFReader** (both compile now). Verified '+'.code==43 + `case 'X'.code:` patterns + stdlib_e2e 12/12.\n\n**REMAINING nue blockers (all cross-module member resolution, the architectural fix darmie chose = \"forward globals + stabilize ids, FQN-keyed\"):**\n- INSTANCE fields on USER classes: GGUFLoader→`TensorInfo.dtype/dims`/`build`/`tensorInfos` (TensorInfo is a secondary class in GGUFReader.hx:277), GQAttention→`KVCache.currentLen`/`useQ8`, LlamaArch→`useQ8`. Fail \"class not registered\" from hir_to_mir.rs:**26810** (instance-field emitter, NO E0100). Root: `import_field_index_map` (compilation.rs:152) is **SymbolId-keyed** and SymbolIds DRIFT across import-set composition (E0100 showed String.code 1161 vs 1158).\n- STATICS: Math.POSITIVE_INFINITY→FPHelper→haxe.io.Input; never forwarded at all (`global_symbol_map` hir_to_mir.rs:125/652 is per-module, never seeded; `lower_hir_to_mir_with_function_map` @37901 takes 22 `external_*` maps but NO global map). Resolution site hir_to_mir.rs:**26470** (HAS E0100).\n- Int64 `high` abstract-property getter; Int32 `this=` (separate, smaller).\n\n**UPDATE 2026-06-22 #2 — the cross-module-forwarding theory was a FALSE TRAIL (darmie: \"dtype is an old claim, resolved; agent read a stale comment\"). PROVEN: 4 minimal toml-repro variants (/tmp/imptest4-7) all show cross-module field/qualified-subtype/method/instance access WORKS. The \"Cannot access field dtype/build/...\" errors were the LOUD-FAIL OVER-REPORTING transient first-pass failures the retry resolves.**\n\n**LOUD-FAIL FIX LANDED** (commit \"fix(import): surface only genuine FINAL import failures\"): try_compile_import eprintln'd on EVERY retry pass; now stashes errors in `last_import_errors` and prints only post-convergence survivors. nue's \"failing\" set dropped 13→4. The TRUE final failures are 4 STDLIB modules: `haxe.Int64` (bare `high`/`low` abstract property in copy(), Int64.hx:42) which CASCADES to `BytesBuffer`+`haxe.io.Output`; and `Int32` (`return this = clamp(...)` abstract this-reassign). **These 4 do NOT cause the nue trap** — /tmp/imptest6/7 have the same 4 and RUN fine; the GGUF read primitive (Bytes.getInt32) works.\n\n**THE ACTUAL nue TRAP — CONFIRMED ROOT:** `nue.loader.GGUFReader.readValue` fails Cranelift codegen (W0020, reason \"return type I64 but encountered Return with no value ... missing lowering of method calls\"). MIR dump shows a `ret void` (value-less return) at `case 7: return Bool(v != 0)`. **The enum variant named `Bool` (MetaValue.Bool, GGUFReader.hx:312/153) collides with the built-in `Bool` TYPE** — in nue's full graph `Bool(x)` resolves to the type (cast/no-op), NOT the MetaValue.Bool constructor → empty return → W0020 → SIGILL. PROVEN: patching `case 7` to a non-`Bool` variant (I8) removes readValue's W0020. CONTEXT-DEPENDENT (faithful readValue copy in /tmp/rvtest2 compiles fine — only fails in nue's full import graph, so the expected-type that should disambiguate the variant isn't propagated there). Enum-variant resolution: ast_lowering.rs:14254 resolve_enum_constructor_from_discriminant + 4880 lower of EnumConstructor; the call-callee `Bool(args)` mis-resolves to the type. After the Bool patch, nue STILL traps 132 on a NEXT blocker (NOT a W0020 — forward-ref stub or runtime, unidentified).\n\n**UPDATE 2026-06-22 #3 — Bool collision FIXED (commit 8f837480).** Root: rayzor flattened enum ARMS into the GLOBAL root scope by BARE name, so an arm named like a builtin type (`MetaValue.Bool` vs builtin `Bool` Abstract) either was skipped (import path) or clobbered the type (main path) → `Bool(x)` resolved to the type → value-less return → readValue W0020. Standard Haxe keeps arms in the enum's namespace (darmie confirmed). Fix (ast_lowering.rs): (a) variant registration ALWAYS creates the arm symbol but only inserts into the root-scope name-map when the slot is FREE (paths ~2409 import + ~2492 main pre-register); (b) call-site collision fix (the `_ =\u003e` arm of lower_call_expression ~9942) extended to ALL type-like callee kinds incl Abstract, matches candidate arms by NAME STRING not InternedString id (they intern differently across contexts!), + same-file fallback: arms sharing one parent enum (qualified-or-bare name) → redirect `Bool(x)` to the constructor. **nue readValue now COMPILES (W0020 gone everywhere).** Verified core_types 25/25, stdlib 12/12, abstract PASS; single + import `Bool(...)` work.\n\n**nue STILL traps 132 at `[load] reading GGUF`** — NO W0020 now, so it's a forward-ref stub from the 4 TRUE stdlib failures being CALLED at runtime: `haxe.Int64` (bare `high`/`low` abstract-property getter in copy(), Int64.hx:42) → cascades to `BytesBuffer`+`haxe.io.Output`; `Int32` (`return this = clamp(...)` abstract this-reassign, Int32.hx:35). The load reaches one (probably via readString `bytes.sub().toString()` or the tensor read). **UPDATE 2026-06-22 #4 — item \"2\" partly done.** The 4 stdlib failures decompose into TWO abstract-compilation bugs:\n- (a) **bare member in an abstract method** (haxe.Int64.copy() reading `high`/`low`, properties declared ~400 lines below copy()) failed \"Cannot find name\". FIXED (commit 867fb356): in an abstract, `this` IS the underlying, so an unresolved bare Ident inside an abstract method synthesizes `this.\u003cname\u003e` and lowers that (ast_lowering.rs Ident arm ~7595). Order-independent. Verified no regression. (Tried + REVERTED an alternative: adding the property to `class_fields` — resolved the name but `this.\u003cprop\u003e` read the storage-less abstract property → SIGSEGV.)\n- (b) **abstract `this = x` reassignment** — THE REMAINING BLOCKER, deeper (HIR/codegen, not TAST). Int64.new() `this = x` compiles but mis-codegens (underlying never set → garbage → SIGSEGV); Int32's `return this = clamp(...)` errors \"Invalid assignment target\". Root: `lower_lvalue` (tast_to_hir.rs:**3234**) handles Variable/Field/ArrayAccess/StaticField but NOT `TypedExpressionKind::This` → `_ =\u003e` \"Invalid assignment target\" (also lowering.rs:1141). Fix needs: make `this` an assignable lvalue for abstracts (it's the underlying value/param) AND the transparent abstract-constructor semantics (`new W(x){this=x}` ⇒ `new W(x)` returns x). Repro /tmp/abstest3.hx (this=x in new() + direct underlying access → SIGSEGV).\n\n**UPDATE 2026-06-22 #5 — MAJOR MILESTONE: ALL nue modules COMPILE now.** Fixed abstract `this = x` reassignment (in-method-body form, commit \"fix(hir): abstract `this = x`\"): `this` in an abstract is the by-value param 0 (`SymbolId(0)`; `HirExprKind::This` reads `symbol_map[SymbolId(0)]`); lower_lvalue (tast_to_hir.rs:3253) had no `This` arm → \"Invalid assignment target\". Added `TypedExpressionKind::This =\u003e HirLValue::Variable(SymbolId::from_raw(0))`. This cleared Int32's `@:op` `return this = clamp(...)` AND the LAST nue import-compile failures — **true import-failure list is now EMPTY, no W0020 anywhere.** (Mapped via workflow wf_2ed72f3f: abstract ctor `new Int64(x){this=x}` already value-wraps to x at hir_to_mir.rs:18187 — DON'T add ctor-body lowering. `this`-as-lvalue gated would be cleaner — currently also permits class `this=`, crash-free.)\n\n**nue NOW traps at RUNTIME (132 udf #0xc11f trap stub), NOT compile.** lldb: a forward-ref stub HIT at runtime; JIT frames don't unwind. RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS shows 4 stranded GENERIC stdlib constructors (empty forward-ref bodies, ids 2x0004): `haxe.iterators.ArrayIterator.new`, `ArrayKeyValueIterator.new`, `haxe.ds.TreeNode.new`, `haxe.ds.BalancedTree.new` — their NON-ctor methods resolve, only the constructors strand (fixup_stale_constructor_ids @compilation.rs:4007 rewrites the map by name but the desugared `new X()` call in user MIR keeps the stale id). nue calls one at runtime → trap. Repro: /tmp/stt (Map + for-in via toml). ISOLATED 3 distinct pre-existing bugs: (1) array for-in works (ArrayIterator stub installed but NOT hit). (2) **`StringMap.keys()` → Cranelift panic \"can't resolve symbol keys\"** (extern method unbound to runtime; set/get bind, keys doesn't) — repro /tmp/sttC. (3) **key-value `for(k=\u003ev in arr)` miscompiles to 0** — repro /tmp/sttB. nue's 132 trap is the stranded BalancedTree/TreeNode ctor (sttB key-value miscompiles not traps, so not that; StringMap.keys panics at COMPILE not runtime, so not that).\n\n**UPDATE 2026-06-22 #6 — stranded generic constructors FIXED (commit \"fix(link): resolve stranded generic stdlib constructors by qualified name\").** Root: the stub→real fixup (compilation.rs ~3860 `unique_bare_match`) matched a forward-ref trap stub to its real def by BARE name + signature requiring a UNIQUE match. Every class ctor is bare `new` and many share sig `(*void)-\u003evoid` → ambiguous → gave up → real `BalancedTree.new`/`ArrayIterator.new`/`TreeNode.new` stranded behind their stubs → `new BalancedTree()` SIGILL'd. Fix: carry each candidate's FULL qualified name (3rd tuple element) and disambiguate by qname FIRST (qname pins class, sig pins overload), fall back to unique bare match. Repro /tmp/sttD (`new BalancedTree()` via toml): 132→resolved. core_types 25/25.\n\n**nue trap MOVED again: now an LLVM-BACKEND crash on a tier-promoted function.** With `enable_tier_promotion=true` + llvm-backend feature, hot functions promote to LLVM (EXPECTED per darmie). The now-resolved generic ctor (BalancedTree etc.) promotes → LLVM produces invalid IR → **`module.verify()` (llvm_jit_backend.rs:1065) crashes building its Twine error message** (`llvm::Twine::printOneChild` EXC_BAD_ACCESS addr=0xc3, no unwind past frame#0). Per-function verify (999, `verify(true)`) PASSES — it's a MODULE-level/cross-fn issue. The promoted fn that fails verify then becomes a trap-stub → 132 (or the verify crashes → 139; both seen). Crash happens BEFORE the RAYZOR_DUMP_LLVM_IR dump (1042), so the bad IR can't be captured without per-fn-dump-before-verify instrumentation. Clean repro: /tmp/sttD (BalancedTree, default toml = LLVM tier on) → lldb shows the Twine crash. NEXT: find which promoted fn the LLVM backend mis-compiles (instrument compile_module_bodies to dump each fn's IR before adding to the module, or verify each fn individually with verify(false) to name it) → fix the LLVM-backend codegen of generic ctors / that fn. SEPARATE: StringMap.keys() unbound (Cranelift \"can't resolve symbol keys\", /tmp/sttC); key-value `for(k=\u003ev)` →0 (/tmp/sttB); mixed enum-variant array literals fail. **Committed this session (9): multi-pass-retry, Ptr/Usize, eval, char-magic, loud-fail-final-survivors, Bool-collision, abstract-this-bare-member, abstract-this-assign, stranded-ctor-link.** See [[project_haxe_q4_dot_phase3]].\n\n**UPDATE 2026-06-23 #7 — nue native load SIGILL ROOT = runtime-intrinsic forward-ref STUB trap; FIXED (committed 15d73a7d, \"fix(link): rebind runtime-intrinsic forward-ref stubs to their extern symbol\").** At HEAD (1945a78d, LLVM21) nue llama-chat SIGILLs (exit 132, udf #0xc11f) in `[load] reading GGUF`. lldb+FN_PTRS pinned it: `Main.main → GGUFLoader.loadWithTokenizer → GGUFReader.parse(+0x4f0) → haxe_bytes_sub` (a TRAP STUB), NOT the BalancedTree/iterator ctors (those stubs install but are never hit). ROOT: `register_stdlib_mir_forward_ref` (hir_to_mir.rs:6786) builds its stub via `IrControlFlowGraph::new()` which is NOT empty — it seeds ONE entry block with the default `Unreachable` terminator (blocks.rs:237). Codegen keys `is_extern` off `cfg.blocks.is_empty()` (cranelift_backend.rs:1337/688), so a 1-block stub is NOT seen as an extern → skipped at definition → the finalize safety net installs a `udf` trap. For a stdlib MIR *wrapper* the real body merges in later; but for a C-ABI RUNTIME symbol (`haxe_bytes_sub/get/set/to_string`, `haxe_string_char_code_at_ptr`, registered in runtime/src/plugin_impl.rs via `register_symbol!`) there's no body to merge → it stays a trapping stub. The SAME symbol ALSO appears as a real 0-block extern in another per-module builder (that copy binds fine via declare_function's `Import \u003cname\u003e` path, cranelift_backend.rs:1566-1578, reusing the runtime FuncId at 1491-1510). FIX (compilation.rs `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs`, after the stub/real-func maps): rebind a forward-ref stub to its extern symbol — CLEAR its `cfg.blocks` so `is_extern` holds — gated to names that ALSO exist as a true 0-block extern (proof it's a runtime symbol) AND have NO real (non-stub, non-empty) body. Removes the rebound ids from `stub_by_id` so the CallDirect rewrite leaves them pointing at the now-extern fn. 6 stubs rebound for nue. RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS prints `[rebind-extern] N …`. Regression: haxe suite **149/151, 0 crash** (2 pre-existing: test_copy_clone_identity flaky exit-133 [[bugs_test_copy_clone_identity_flaky]] confirmed 1/4; test_balancedtree_crossmodule_mono = EnumValueMap compile-error, upstream of this post-load pass). GOTCHA en route: `target/release/libnue_plugins.dylib` was MISSING (build churn) → plugin symbol-map unloaded → `KvCacheQ8.alloc` static-extern emitted as bare `alloc`; rebuilt via `CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0 cargo build -p nue-plugins --release`.\n\n**UPDATE 2026-06-23 #8 — BLOCKER #2 (cross-module `.toString()` mis-dispatch) FIXED (committed 348f9b7b, \"fix(tast): recover declared method type for typeless cross-module receivers\").** ROOT (pinned by instrumenting find_by_name + resolve_method_function_id + get_stdlib_runtime_info + infer_method_call_return_type, plus a 4-agent workflow): `bytes.sub(pos,len)` returns **Dynamic** cross-module. `infer_method_call_return_type` (ast_lowering.rs:13064): the bound `sub` method symbol has an INVALID type_id (a cross-module/typedef typeless placeholder — `rayzor.Bytes` is an `extern class` reached via the `haxe.io.Bytes` typedef, whose method-signature return types are NOT established on import; ALL `Bytes.sub` symbols are typeless, recv `this.bytes` field is even TypeId::invalid()), so it falls to `infer_builtin_method_type` which has Array/String/EReg arms but **no Bytes arm** → `_ =\u003e dynamic_type()`. Then `.toString()` on that Dynamic: the `.toString` FIELD symbol is correct (`Bytes.toString`) but with a Dynamic receiver the resolution decays and lands on `sys_net_Host_toString` (fn730286) — NOT via find_by_name's suffix rule (find_by_name never even sees a Host class; the dotted `haxe.io.Bytes`→None gap then lets a wrong-symbol fallback win). Garbage HaxeString → all GGUF meta keys decode to the same garbage → \"missing meta key general.architecture\". FIX (ast_lowering.rs, 2 parts): (1) `infer_method_call_return_type` — when the bound method symbol is typeless, FIRST recover the real declared method from the receiver class via `resolve_class_method_symbol` (already handles typedef/phantom-class, only returns valid-typed) and use its return type; (2) `infer_builtin_method_type` Class arm — add Bytes: `sub`/`slice`→receiver Bytes type, `toString`/`getString`→String (completes the existing builtin inference; the recovery in (1) finds nothing because extern Bytes has NO typed method anywhere). Verified: nue now reads GGUF metadata correctly; haxe suite 148/151 0-crash (debug_checksum 137 was a concurrent-load timeout, passes 3/3 standalone; test_balancedtree+test_copy pre-existing). darmie directive this session: \"no more fallbacks that hide problems\" — so this fixes the root (lost type) rather than loosening the resolution fallback. **nue then advances to BLOCKER #3: `GGUFReader.metaInt` SIGSEGVs (EXC_BAD_ACCESS 0x800) in `haxe_unbox_int_ptr` — the MetaValue enum `case U8(x)|...|I64(x): x` payload is unboxed as a pointer. CROSS-MODULE-ONLY (minimal /tmp/enumtest.hx multi-pattern Int enum works in-file). SAME architectural class as #2: import-compiled modules lose types (methods, enum payloads, fields → Dynamic/invalid). Full native nue load = a CHAIN of these = the multi-session \"forward globals + stabilize ids\" architectural fix; per-construct band-aids (like the Bytes arm) are whack-a-mole darmie pushed back on.**\n\n**(historical) nue advanced past the SIGILL to BLOCKER #2 (cross-module method mis-dispatch, NOT a trap — clean Haxe throw).** `GGUFReader.readString()` does `bytes.sub(pos,len).toString()` (GGUFReader.hx:126); instrumented: `len`/`pos`/`bytes.sub` all CORRECT (subLen=20, rawb='gen…'), but `.toString()` lowers to `call fn730286` = **`@sys_net_Host_toString`** instead of `haxe_bytes_to_string` → returns a malformed HaxeString (garbage ptr, len=7) → all 36 meta keys read as the same garbage → `meta.get(\"general.architecture\")==null` → throw \"missing meta key\". CROSS-MODULE ONLY: minimal /tmp/substr_test.hx (`Bytes.alloc(5); b.sub(0,3).toString()`, NO toml/imports) returns the CORRECT content 'hi!'. Root: the receiver's `Bytes` type (haxe.io.Bytes `typedef = rayzor.Bytes`, Bytes.hx:27; sub():Bytes) is lost across the import boundary, so toString's `class_symbol` at hir_to_mir.rs:22556-22605 resolves to the wrong class and `find_by_name(class_name,\"toString\")` picks Host.toString. THIS IS THE ARCHITECTURAL cross-module type/member-resolution bug (the \"forward globals + stabilize ids\" multi-session work) — NOT yet fixed. Side-note: `String.length` returns `null` on a freshly bytes-decoded string even in-file (/tmp/substr_test.hx, separate minor bug).\n\nkills that class's OWN registration\nAdding `public static function dumpPlan()` to `nue.Q4Matmul` failed with\n`E0100 Cannot access field 'dumpPlan': class not registered` — AND cascaded\nE0803 ambiguous-field errors onto unrelated classes\n(`GenerationConfig.temperature` vs `LocalTempSampler.temperature`, etc).\nThe trigger was NOT the signature (a `SpinPool` param was my first suspect and\nremoving it changed nothing) — it was the BODY calling\n`nue.Linear.useHaxeMatmul()`, a cross-module STATIC. Dropping that one call\nmade the class register and all cascaded errors vanish.\n**Rule of thumb until the cluster is fixed: a public static's body must not\ncall another module's static.** Same family as `matmulQ8_0`'s existing\nexplicit-`sp` note. Cost ~20 min to isolate; the error points at the wrong\nclass, so bisect the BODY, not the signature.\n\ncross-module static VARIABLE yields empty, silently\nTwo files, no GGUF, no nue. `probe/Consts.hx`:\n```haxe\npackage probe;\nclass Consts {\n    public static inline var INLINE_STR = \"abc\";\n    public static var PLAIN_STR = \"def\";\n    public static inline var INLINE_INT = 42;\n    public static var PLAIN_INT = 43;\n}\n```\n`Main.hx` (default package, `class-paths=[\".\"]`) reads each and prints it:\n\n| declaration | value read from another module |\n|---|---|\n| `static inline var INLINE_INT = 42` | **42** — correct |\n| `static inline var INLINE_STR = \"abc\"` | **empty** |\n| `static var PLAIN_STR = \"def\"` | **empty** |\n| `static var PLAIN_INT = 43` | **empty** — `Sys.println(int)` prints nothing |\n\nOnly the inline INT survives, because it is constant-folded at compile time and\nnever performs a real cross-module read. Everything else reads empty.\n\n**Two things make this vicious:** it is SILENT (no E0100, no warning, the\nprogram runs), and a broken value POISONS THE WHOLE CONCATENATION — \n`Sys.println(\"len=\" + a.length)` prints an empty line rather than `len=0`, so\nthe diagnostic you add to investigate also disappears. Bisect by printing the\nvalue ALONE, never inside a concat.\n\nFound via `new ChatTemplate(ChatTemplate.CHATML)` in user code silently\nbecoming `kind=\"\"` (→ wrong template). nue's own code is unaffected — it never\nreads another module's static var (verified by grep) — so this hits USERS of\nthe public API, not nue internals. Matches the long-standing \"STATICS: never\nforwarded at all (`global_symbol_map` is per-module, never seeded)\" item above;\nthis is that bug, reduced to 8 lines. Fix direction remains FQN forward-globals,\nper [[feedback_fqn_first_resolution]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_import_xmodule_member_resolution` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}